WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.710
Do you know enough about this technology to have a serious debate?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:04.739
Is that a trick question?

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:13.860
This is the first time Congress is having a hearing about cryptocurrencies.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:21.440
Oh, that explains why crypto is doing so well until now. As policymakers, we need to understand what we're talking about here.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:30.440
We haven't done before, but I'm game. When I'm trying to wrap my head around a new topic like cryptocurrencies, I try to relate it back to something I understand, like baseball.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:36.770
All of the complex topics that fall under the purview of Congress can be boiled down to a folksy baseball analogy.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.610
But there's something about crypto that scares people.&nbsp; The call is coming from inside the House of Representatives.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.009
There are concerns with the use of cryptocurrencies for nefarious activities.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.590
You know what else is used for nefarious activities?

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.939
Bottle rockets, nunchucks, ninja throwing stars.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.270
The Patriot Act, my ex-wife.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.579
Subpoena power, wet markets.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.729
Slim congressional majority.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.749
Time Travel Paradox.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.520
A 78 Buick Riviera. Was for me.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.649
Well, my coat dealer doesn't accept Venmo.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.089
I've got several questions for you and then one of them will be from completely left field.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:13.379
Oh, baseball, what threats or benefits to a blockchain system will come from quantum computing?

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.150
That's a weirdly good question.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.529
Well, we'll see what happens there.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.090
That's a weirdly bad answer.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.889
What makes Web3 different is the ability to own the actual network.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.019
Do I need a baseball metaphor to understand this?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.079
The lack of clarity is clear.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:34.379
The industry has the potential to improve a lot of people's lives.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.329
I'm sure we'll find a way to stop that.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.930
Unfortunately, it would only take a few misguided curveballs, we'll say...

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.170
Baseball! ...from Washington to undo some of the progress.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.389
Now, Congress should not be dumb enough...

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.680
It is. ... to raise a red flag around this technology revolution.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:02:00.239
Reading crypto as a single unitary activity whose main feature is a need for financial regulation would be like treating the original internet in the 1990s as primarily a tax policy issue.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.230
Don't give 'em ideas.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:09.709
We believe a user-controlled decentralized internet is better than an internet largely controlled by five big companies. Or a government-controlled by two big political parties.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.800
The biggest critics of cryptocurrency have been the biggest banks.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:21.599
Elizabeth Banks. It is true you try to use a credit card or debit card to buy a sandwich today,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.849
the system takes a percent, half a percent, 50 cents away from the merchant.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.069
Half a percent is 50 cents.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.389
You're buying $100 sandwiches.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.059
You try to use crypto to buy a sandwich today.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.259
I don't know where you can go in Washington to use a bitcoin to buy a sandwich.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:38.610
Can't be done at all.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.279
Okay, so that's sort of like a balk.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.020
Now, that's not a fair comparison.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.050
I don't understand baseball.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.149
I just want to regulate it.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.340
There's still extreme volatility, it seems, in this marketplace.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:01.289
Yeah, it goes up and down and up and down while the dollar only goes down.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.860
DeFi is the most dangerous part of the crypto world. Let me guess:

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:12.569
The solution is to give more power and money to unelected experts who share all of your cultural preconceptions,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.360
watch The Handmaid's Tale, and went to the right school.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:24.060
Is that — that the right thing? Bitcoin could be displaced by Ether, which could be first placed by Doge, which should be replaced by a Hamster coin?

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.979
And then there's Cobra coin.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.430
And what could mongoose coin do to crypto coin?

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.779
Are you trying to say mongoose?

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.479
Will Mongoose coin always have a value?

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:33.930
I don't know.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:34.650
I just made it up.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.189
It's a joke.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.379
I don't think you know how jokes work.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:43.680
We often hear that crypto industry is the wild, wild west, where there is no regulation guiding the industry.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.770
Do any of you feel unregulated? That's a little  personal, don't you think?

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.059
I have satisfied myself.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.719
For the record, I'm not doing anything with that.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.639
OK, I'm just letting that be.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.729
I'm not a monster.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:59.129
Had the government got in the way of the internet, we would still be using dial-up today.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.439
You realize they still use fax machines here, right?

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:11.620
The central bank needs the ability to match the amount of money in the system to the needs of the economy. Or just to inflate the value away if the government wants to keep spending but doesn't wanna raise taxes.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.770
You think you could sell metal coins and say U.S.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:14.759
dollar coin on them?

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.170
I'm a pretty good salesman.

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.860
I think I could sell that. We'll buy anything around here. Like how many we're talking about, like 20,000 units?

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.059
I could push 20,000 units.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:21.689
I could sell you that.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:27.089
So I guess I'm left incredibly confused. And I'm hoping that we do nothing.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.680
That might be the answer.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.070
Good plan.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:29.699
Let's do that.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.800
This meeting is adjourned.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:32.960
Hey!

